[[!toc levels=5]]

LLM-generated summary: This lecture presents a physics-based framework for understanding complex systems as open dissipative structures driven by high throughput of energy, matter, or information. The speaker defines such systems by the emergence of reproducible statistical properties (fractal dimension, fixed fractions of endpoints and branch points, loop avoidance, power minimization) alongside history-dependent, chaotic features that are sensitive to initial conditions. Experiments with high-voltage ball-bearing assemblies in castor oil illustrate self-assembly, Hebbian learning, and the coexistence of predictable and unpredictable observables; these observations are linked to broader implications for atomic-scale neural nets, mixed-reality interfaces, and the possible emergence of superhuman levels of abstraction once neuron counts exceed biological limits by many orders of magnitude.

# Becoming a Physicist

So I study chaos and I like chaos. So when other people start to run away, like in fear or anger, I pick up my notebook and start taking notes and start to enjoy things. So what I am going to do today is to share with you my intuition when I have to run too. Like complex systems, people like to study forest fires. So if everybody is running in this direction, we go in this direction towards the fire. But at some point we have to run too. So to find out when you really have to run, this is what we are doing. And we try to forecast, predict catastrophes, earthquakes, explosions, all the things a little boy dreams of doing.

Okay, and so I am very fortunate that I am sort of paid to play for all of my life. So this is sort of a good, nice secret, which we should not share too often, that if you want to be playing for all of your life, you should become a physicist.

