# Physics Definition of a Complex System

And now I share my favorite definition. A complex system is a system with a large throughput. And this throughput could be energy, it could be a fluid flow, so something comes in, but then if you put something into the system, something has to come out too. So what we do is in order to make a system complex we ramp up the throughput till something spectacular happens inside and this spectacular is called an emergent structure. Scientifically those systems are often called open dissipative systems. Open means you put something in. Dissipative means something else is coming out. Open dissipative systems. Systems with a large throughput.

Now every area of science of physics, and this is a physics definition, has a key control parameter which is particularly large. Like in astrophysics, distances are very large. In quantum physics, temperatures are very low. In nuclear physics, the energy density is very high. Now, high and low is relative. Like the temperature is low, you call the temperature low whenever quantum phenomena occur. And this could be like 2 million Fahrenheit at a nuclear star, at a star, or this could be a very low temperature compared to human temperature scales for some metal. So large is relative. Large is whenever the flow produces something spectacular.

# Examples of Spectacular Emergent Structures

Now what are those spectacular things? So let me give you some example. For example, if you have energetic chemicals, chemicals, energetic chemicals. Then what can happen is something like a flickering flame. And so we study flickering flames. And actually I brought a candle, I like candles because flickering flames are really cool for many reasons. So just to illustrate that, I brought a candle. I hope we do not set off the sprinkler. Okay, that is small enough, should be okay. And if flow, we have chaos and that is cool too. Okay.

So, another flow could be, for example, a fluid flow. And if the fluid flow is large, you get turbulence. If the relative motion of tectonic plates is large, tectonic plates, then we get earthquakes and other earthquakes. If the electrical current is large, we get lightning. And so forth. So if the traffic is large, we get stop-and-go motion. If the tension is large, which is a more abstract flow, we get fracture. If the fluid flow is large, we get turbulence. If the heat flow is large, we get boiling. If there is a lot of rain and snow, we get avalanches. If there is enough food, sufficient amount of food, we get life. And if the information flow is sufficiently large, you get computer crashes. Okay.

Now, traffic is not necessarily a complex system, because if you look at, say, interstate, at 2 a.m., you get one car, and then you get nothing for a long time. You get another car, and then you get nothing. I would not call that a complex system. But if the traffic is large, like during rush hour in Chicago, where the traffic develops some stop-and-go motion, then I will call that a complex system. For the same reason, computers are not necessarily a complex system. Just if you open one application, and then you open a second application, and then you open a third application, and so on, but eventually you may make the computer a complex system, and it behaves in an erratic fashion. A lot of people think that computers are complex systems, because if you have Windows, it becomes erratic already when you open the second application. Okay.

# Consistency with Audience Definitions

So let me now check how well those definitions fit with your definitions, how this definition fits with that definition. By the way, other fields of science have other definitions. Like in computer science, complexity means that it is a complex algorithm. The complexity of an algorithm just means how much computer time you need to evaluate it. In mathematics there are complex numbers which have nothing to do with that definition as far as I can tell. So different fields have different definitions of complex systems, so I am giving you a physics definition of a complex system.

So often the particle which we, the systems which have, we study have many parts, so I may add that. And we look at the patterns which are formed by those many parts. So we often study open dissipative systems with many parts. And in this sense, Kashmir's definition fits. Multiple seemingly random inputs. Outputs, inputs. I do not, those systems often behave in an erratic fashion. So what we do is, those systems behave in a similar way. They can, you can use similar mathematical tools to describe those systems, at least the emergent structures. So the mathematical tools that we use are, for instance, deterministic chaos, which is a mathematical concept, or cellular automata, or fractals, the theory of fractals, or the theory of neural nets, or the theory of genetic algorithms. And we use those mathematical tools to describe properties of the complex system.

And so properties of complex systems are, for instance, that they try to minimize the resistance. So when you push something through like a fluid, the system tries to minimize that power consumption automatically without us doing anything. Or the system, another phenomenon is that the system reacts most sensitive to its own dynamics. If you mirror its own dynamics you get the largest response from the system. Another thing we observed is that those systems when they are self-organized they tend to avoid chaos. So if you let the system alone like this one chaos goes away.

# Video Feedback Experiment

So what this system is is just a video screen and a video camera. And what I do is I take the video signal output from the camera and feed it back to the screen. And then the camera, it takes a picture of the screen. So this goes back in the loop. And so this is an open, dissipative system. Makes interesting patterns like this. So this is life. If you just walk by, you see. But if you let it evolve after a long time, the chaos which you see now fades away. And so those systems tend to avoid chaos. That we developed some statistical methods to predict chaos, and actually this is predictability only probabilistic, so this fits very well. And another phenomenon is that the whole is bigger than the parts because in those systems, often the shape of the container determines the behavior inside. Like if I change the container a little bit, the shape of the container dominates the behavior inside. So, in this sense, the whole is often more important, is more than the sum of the parts.

There are some methods for controlling chaos. Another observation is, by Stephen Wolfram, that discrete math provides often better models than calculus-based models. We observe that fast dynamics often entrain slow dynamics. So if you have two competing agents, and one agent is a bit faster than the other agent, the faster agent usually takes over control. There is a leadership paradigm which says that if you have two competing agents, and one agent wants to impose chaos on the to-be-controlled system, and the other wants to impose order, usually the natural system listens to the agent who wants to impose chaos, and ignores the agent who wants to create order. Why is that? Because orderly behavior is exploitable. Repetitive behavior is exploitable. And therefore the agent who wants to impose chaos usually wins in the competition.

And then what we observe is that those complex systems often do not move smoothly. They sort of jump. They do a stop-and-go motion. So stop-and-go motion, avalanches are very typical for complex systems. So these are some of the things we managed to describe with using these mathematical tools. Okay, so do the definitions fit? They have multiple seemingly random outputs. I would agree with that the dynamics is chaotic, irregular. Like here we have a water wheel. This is a water wheel. Like some water is coming in and the wheel turns to the left or to the right. And imagine you have to predict the next five turns. This is, the smallest vibration in the room can make it turn the other way. And so this is, in this sense, the output is seemingly chaotic. And so this fits. And simple and sometimes unpredictable behavior, like when the wheel is at rest, the behavior is simple, but then it is turning, which is complicated. The reaction of a reaction of a reaction, this is what you see here. And so I agree with this. There is often feedback in the system. Inside this system, there are feedback loops which make it nonlinear. And this is because the throughput is so large, feedback becomes important. Now, it is not simple and not predictable. I will show you today that some aspects of the system are often predictable and the others are not. And we need to find out what aspects of the complex systems are actually predictable. Now, we will show you some experiments on that.

So, a snowflake formation is like formation of a fractal. This is some of the key things we study in complex systems. And because of internal feedback loops, those systems are often nonlinear. So this is feedback. Feedback is important. So throughput is so large that feedback becomes important, and this makes it nonlinear.

