# High-Voltage Ball-Bearing Experiment

So one experiment that I brought, other than the water wheel, is an experiment which has a large electrical current. So let me explain that experiment a little bit. So what we have is here, the experiment consists of two electrodes, a high-voltage electrode and a boundary electrode. So the high-voltage electrode is powered by a battery, and then we have a Petri dish. Inside the Petri dish are a metallic particle, is some castor oil, and this is a boundary electrode which is grounded. So the battery maintains a voltage of about 20,000 volts. The source electrode sprays from the tip charge on the surface of the oil. There is an air gap between the tip and the Petri dish of about 5 centimeters. The diameter is about 12 centimeters, which is about 4 inches. The oil height is about 3 millimeters, which is like 2 tenths of an inch, enough to cover the particles. The oil we use is castor oil because it has a high viscosity, low ohmic heating, and is biodegradable. You can eat this experiment if you want. Most of our experiments are edible. Okay, this is important because they are often so messy so you do not want to handle poisonous stuff. If you do a messy experiment the particles are non-magnetic stainless steel and they have a diameter of 1.6 millimeters. Okay and now I am showing you what you get.

So let me switch the Okay so I have I start out with random distribution of particles. Okay, I will take a clean sheet of paper. Okay, and then I switch on the non-equilibrium, back the voltage, and then I try to make it complex. So right now, I would need a volunteer. Here? Okay. Yes, okay, please come down. So please raise the voltage in steps of 1,000 volts till you see something moving. Is there anything moving? So this is not a complex system yet. It is sort of regular physics. Anything moving? No. So this is how you make a system complex. You ramp up the throughput until something spectacular happens. Or the experimenter turns pale. Making noises. Making noises. That is a good sign. So ramp it up a bit more. Moving. Yes, okay, ramp it up a little bit more. Oh, okay, then we have to wait.

Okay, so you see that the particles agglomerate to tree-like structures. So trees are self-assembling, so you get self-assembling structures, stable, like if you break one of those structures, like a small gap here, and then I feed it again with energy, you see it is repairing, these are stable self-assembling structures. Of course if you make a big perturbation like this it does not grow back to the same structure. It grows a different structure. But if you count the number of endpoints you always get the same number of endpoints.

So let me describe that real quick here. So if you get a structure like this, you have three different types of particles. We have the particles at the end which have only one neighbor, like the red ones. And then we have the particles, the trunks, which touch, have two neighbors, like here. These are trunks. And then we have branching points, which have three neighbors. So we classify all particles in the structure into three classes, endpoints, branching points, and the rest. And when you run this experiment, you find that no matter how many ball bearings you have, no matter how much oil, no matter what material the ball bearings are, as long as they are conducting, you always get 22 percent endpoints and 22 percent branching points. And the rest makes connections. So this is very reproducible. So this system has some properties which are very reproducible. For instance, that the number of endpoints is always 22 percent. Another reproducible property is that this system does not form closed loops. Do you see any closed loop in the experiment? Okay, to check that the system indeed avoids closed loops, I make a closed loop. Like this. And then I power it again. And you see it opens up. This system detects closed loops and this is a reproducible, a remarkable reproducible property too. When you look outside and study the branches of a tree or the root system of a plant you will almost never see closed loops. And so the question is why does this structure form no closed loops? One of the answers is that almost all the current enters through the endpoints. And so when you break up a closed loop, you create two more endpoints, and this lowers the resistance. And this is another important reproducible quantity. If you run this experiment many times and you measure the resistance between the input electrodes, the red one and the output electrodes of this one and this one, you find no matter what you do to the experiment you always get the same limiting resistance. And during the experiment the system tries to reduce the resistance. Reducing the resistance means minimizing the power consumption. So this system has a tendency, if you let it, to minimize power consumption. This is cool, is it not? So let us say if you would let your car optimize itself, it will become more energy efficient. If you let your washing machine optimize itself, it will become more energy efficient. So there is a natural tendency to become more energy efficient and Ilya Prigogine discovered this in the 1970s of the last century and he won a Nobel Prize for that. He discovered there is a natural tendency for complex systems to minimize the energy consumption. Nobel Prize in complex systems, Ilya Prigogine.

So in this system, there are reproducible properties. But there are other things which are not reproducible. And for instance, if you disturb the system, like if I choose a slightly different initial condition, or if I choose a different initial condition and I would move one of the ball bearings a tiny little bit, I get a different structure. So the system is sensitive to the initial conditions. The trees which are forming are never the same, and even if you try to make it the same, even if you try to put the ball bearings exactly at the same position, you do not get the same trees. Like the branching pattern is different, except that we always get 22 percent endpoints and 22 percent branching points.

By the way, biological systems tend to have the same property. If you know the soil condition and the climate at a certain spot on the earth, you can predict the leaf area. Like if you would measure how the green area of the leaves depends only on the soil condition. And now those leaves could be on a couple of tall old trees or they could be on grass or they could be on more smaller trees. But the leaf area depends only on the soil condition and the climate. And the leaves can be on different types of vegetation. So the type of vegetation, the size of the trees, that is history dependent. But the leaf area is not. The leaf area depends. Like in our experiments, the number of endpoints does not depend on the history, but some other things are, like the exact structure of the trees. So sensitivity to the initial condition means chaos.

# Double-Pendulum Demonstration of Chaos

