# Hebbian Learning and Self-Assembling Wires

Okay, next I want to show you, I want to shift gears, and I want to show you one other property of this system. So now I move most of the particles on the side. I lower the electrode. And I mark two points on the paper, A and B. These are the points A and B. And I put a few ball bearings between A and B, like this, a few points. And I try to move the rest of the ball bearings out of the way so they do not interfere too much. Okay, and then what I do is I connect electrode B first, and I ramp up the voltage. And what you should see is that there is a wire forming. You see the wire forming? So this is a self-assembling wire. You may even see a little bit of lightning in between. And then I connect the other electrode, and you see another wire forming. So if I use electrode A a little bit, and then I use electrode B a little bit, you see you get a branched wire. Now if I use only B, if I use only electrode B, all the ball bearings are moving over, and the connection to electrode A is destroyed. If I use only, let me reduce the number of ball bearings here a little bit so introduce more competition. So if I connect B only connection A is destroyed. If I connect A only, B is destroyed. So if I use B a little bit, and then I use A a lot, then B a little bit, and then A a lot, you see the connection to electrode A is well formed, has many ball bearings, whereas the connection to the other electrode, which I am using only occasionally, is not that well formed. This is called Hebb's learning rule.

In your hippocampus, you grow every day about 10,000 neurons. If you use them, they stay. If you do not, they will die within two weeks. And if you lose one of your neurons a lot, it is strengthened. That is Hebb's learning rule. Hebb discovered that many years ago. And so what I am showing you here is that this system satisfies Hebb's learning rule. So this means, if it satisfies Hebb's learning rule, this means we could potentially build a brain out of that one.

# Building a Perceptron and Defining Understanding

Okay, so how would we build a brain? Well, we would build a simple perceptron. A perceptron is like this. So you have a couple of inputs, like you have one, zero, one as an input. And you want to have as an output, let us say, one, zero, zero. And then you put a box with ball bearings in between. And the one means the electrode is connected. So the zero means not connected, and the one means connected. And so the connection is, let us say, to five volts at the output side, connected means grounded. There is a grounded electrode. Not connected means zero. Zero, not connected. And then you put a whole bunch of ball bearings in between. And so what will happen if you have those two electrodes connected, the ball bearings will form a wire connection like this. What you do then is you may apply a different input pattern and a different output pattern and repeat the input patterns and the output patterns and the system can learn. That is a perceptron.

