Now there are, I do not think it is my phone, there are different levels of understanding. Like there is a level of understanding of a first year of a foreign language, first year foreign language. This is like a dictionary, a dictionary level of a foreign language. And like a C. elegans would have that. And let me plot here in this direction the number of neurons. And C. elegans has about 300 neurons, which corresponds to sort of a handheld calculator. C. elegans. And so the C. elegans has sort of a dictionary level of understanding. Can process certain inputs, has probably is a little warm, 300 neurons, like a mosquito. And then probably the C. elegans has no feeling for time and has no feeling for left and right. Can go in a certain direction. Then there is a second year of a foreign language where people learn grammar. They can have a match, can process many more inputs, infinitely more inputs. It is like a difference between one and two dimensions. And this would, like, a C compiler could do this, C++, this level of understanding. And then there are other levels of understanding, like a third or fourth year of a foreign language. And this includes context history being able to translate a poem. And this is what humans can do. Computers are not quite there in terms of compute power. And humans have about 30 billion neurons. And then you can do...computers will be there in about 5 to 10 years. But they are not there in terms of compute power yet. So there are different levels of understanding, and there are sort of infinities between those levels in terms of processing capability. But it is still translation. And so when being able to translate, if this means understanding, this is something which these machines, the perceptron can do very well. It translates an input into an output.

# Atomic Neural Nets and Future Implications

Okay, so, you could say, so what? We build another brain. There is only one difference. And the difference is that the particles which I was plotting there could be nanoparticles, which have a diameter, a factor of a thousand thinner than your neurons. So if this is a human neuron, so let us say this is a long human neuron, complicated neuron, this is the neuron, human neuron. You can fit inside a human neuron a thousand by a thousand by a thousand atomic neural net neurons. Because the human neuron has a diameter of about one micrometer. Whereas an atomic neural net neuron has a diameter of one nanometer, which is a factor of a thousand thinner. And so therefore you can fit into the volume, the space which is occupied by one human neuron, a billion atomic neural net neurons.

Now how about power consumption? Well the power consumption depends to first order on say resistance. Now our human tissue is not a particularly good conductor. Like not even a metal. So the energy consumption of our brain is about 60 watts but it could be much less. Because our human brain is not a good conductor. Now you can build those perceptrons, you can use for ball bearings, and actually one of my students is doing this, he uses as particles in this experiment superconducting nanoparticles, which have a much smaller energy consumption than the human tissue. So what I am saying is we could build now with current technology a brain which is the size of a human brain but has a billion times more neurons and spends much less energy. Would not that be cool?

Okay so let us go back where are we on that plot? Okay so this is 30 billion and this is 300. And so the atomic neural net is sort of off the scale over here. Atomic neural net. Because it is a billion, a billion can have a billion times 30 billion neurons. And we can only speculate what will happen then. So probably there are other levels of understanding up there which are incomprehensible for humans. What does it mean being incomprehensible for humans? Oh, so just imagine the C. elegans or a mosquito would ask you, or would ask me, to teach her calculus or to teach her physics. The mosquito will say, I want to have, I want to take physics 214 or 212, physics introductory course. What would I tell the mosquito? I would say, sorry little animal, this is a very noble effort that you want to learn physics, but you do not have the brain. You do not have enough with 300 neurons, come on. Okay so the relation between us and the mosquito is like the relation between the atomic neural net and us. So if an atomic neural net would make a prediction say OK I come to the lab and I switch on the computer or look at the computer. And the atomic neural net would say, I have a suggestion for you. Do this experiment today. Why should I do this experiment? The atomic neural net would say, because last night, I read 100,000 physics papers, which were published this year, and checked what is relevant to your research. And I checked the literature for the last 20 years, too, a million papers. And I found an interesting correlation between the data you measured yesterday and data published in that paper 10 years ago. And therefore, you should do this experiment. Would I do it? Probably. So the research would not be driven anymore by humans, but by an atomic neural net. To a certain extent, that has happened already. Because when I started a new project, I checked Wikipedia. I checked Google first before I start the project. But so far, Wikipedia cannot read research papers. And this may change in the next five or ten years. So atomic neural nets may use their compute capability to do something which is unimaginable.

Will they be friendly? OK. So I can imagine, like, they will need us to build them. Like we need cows for milk. So we are friendly to cows, is it not? Usually, sort of. Sometimes. OK, so this is one thing that will be friendly, hopefully. Can we avoid building them? I am not sure. That is atomic, and actually one of my graduate students was hired to build them by Cypress chip manufacturer with a specific task to build atomic neural nets. So we can just hope that atomic neural nets will be friendly to us. Are you friendly to mosquitoes? Sometimes. Or no? But we are okay with C. elegans, I would say. So this is one thing.

And now the other thing is that probably we will be immersed in a world which is combined out of those super intelligent systems and the real world. So right now, I think the picture will change. Right now, the picture is we are controlling the world. So this is the world and this is us. This is us and so we try to control the world. We try at least. I think the picture will change. This is the world, this is us, and these are those super intelligent systems. And so we are much more part of the world. We are sort of sadly changing sides. We are on the other side. And so the world as a whole will be a combination of the real world and the virtual world created by super intelligent systems.

How far are we with that? Okay, so let me show you last, it will take maybe two or three more minutes to show you something. So we are, in our lab, actually studying this. So what would happen if you combine the real world with the virtual world, like a real pendulum, like this one, with a virtual pendulum, an instantaneous bidirectional coupling. So those super intelligent systems would be on the alert and controlling us and taking data all the time. They are sort of fully integrated with the real world. So we expect that some new interesting states will emerge, which are sort of a mixture of the real world and the virtual world. We study those systems.

And one experiment which I think is really thought-provoking is the following. If you play a computer game which is very realistic where the people look like the real world people and where the houses look like the real houses we predict that there is a transition where the player can no longer distinguish between the events in the game and the events in the real world. So what we predict is that that boundary, that boundary between the real world and the virtual world will go away. And so, and actually, I brought an experiment, this is my last experiment, where we can sort of demonstrate this, hopefully. And this is this experiment. And I would need a volunteer, but the volunteer should be mentally stable and strong-willed, because it is sometimes a bit challenging. So I need, I mean, you could feel sick afterwards. So who is willing to do that experiment? Only, so what we will do is we will try to give you an out-of-body experience. So who wants to get an, yes, please come down. Okay, so the experiment goes like this. Out-of-body experience is sort of a mixed reality set. So please sit down on that chair and look at the screen. Okay. And so what I will do is I will videotape you from behind. Okay.

So you can see yourself now from behind and then I will tap you on the shoulder like this. Can you see yourself from behind? So where are you? I... So, do you feel something strange? No. Okay. What is your name? Katarina. Katarina, okay. Oh, yeah, Katarina. Okay. Thank you, Katarina. Let try somebody else. So what you show what I want you to do is that you have suddenly confused where you are. Like usually people think their identity is somewhere between their eyes. And if you can see yourself from behind and move you, see how you move from behind, suddenly you think you are outside your body. Now with this little setup, with this simple setup, it happens only in one out of three cases. But if people use video goggles, like 3D video goggles, it happens almost ever. So I would need another volunteer. Let us try it again. One more. Volunteer. Yes, please come down. So this is sort of the third-person perspective in a video game. So please sit down. Okay, and so I am videotaping you from behind. Like this. And so you can see yourself from behind now. Oops. I think maybe this is the problem. You cannot really see yourself. I need to be a little bit closer. Okay. I think I need to zoom out and focus. The color is important. It is important that 3D is much better than 2D. Okay. So can you see yourself from behind? How do you feel? Almost. Okay, almost. Not much. Okay. Well, what is your name? Vijay. Thank you, Vijay. Okay, so what can happen is that sometimes people have the feeling they can see themselves from behind, behind, which makes them confused, and sometimes people are even throwing up afterwards. And so it is a very strong, it can be a very strong sensation, seeing yourself from behind, and then you do not know where you are, you are confused.

