Q: Any other questions? Or then we will... That is one. Oh, okay. We have intelligent systems, sort of, that we talked to using a limited bulk category. For example, when you are making a reservation for airlines. Yes. See the human language even computer languages go beyond human languages in many ways because in human languages we cannot nest. And in computer languages we can nest inside a loop and inside a loop. So I think computer languages go in many ways beyond what human languages are doing, but in some other ways they are not going yet that far. Like they are not really context dependent, but they maybe catch up to become context dependent. So I think human language is restricted by the human hardware of the human brain, and you you can build systems which go beyond, which do not have those restrictions. Like in terms of what you can memorize the sentences can have only a certain length. So I think human language is only relevant in the sense that we can interact with the system. So third of the super intelligent system could have some thoughts which are incomprehensible to us, but if they want to talk to us, they have to boil it down to a language which we understand. But this does not mean they are restricted to that. But it also has to understand the real world. And that is the hard part. It is putting the real world into its knowledge. Yeah, but we have to model the real world with equations which contain at most seven variables because we cannot comprehend more. So the super intelligent system may come up with modeling techniques which are far more efficient than what we have. Because we are limited by human thinking. So the super intelligent system may be able to make much better predictions and find much more correlations in the world surrounding us than we can do. I just hope the real world does not get reduced down to a smaller level so that the super intelligent system can manage it. I do not think we do this in the same way. The super system is part of the real world too. So it just the difference of flow in the real world.

Q: Two more questions. Cashier right. So your explanation of understanding was translation. Yes. What you showed there seems to imply it is a matter of constructing an inverse function in some ways. That if you receive an inverse, but then you are able to go back and construct what would have sent out that signal that you received, we are basically constructing an inverse function. Yes. Yes. And the higher levels of understanding seem to then be basically constructing not just the inverse function, but an associated set of facts that may be associated with the inverse function. So that context, history, and all that kind of stuff. So when you then talk about higher levels of understanding, I am not sure I really buy that because our understanding, which is different from the. Yes. What are facts associated with that input? Is there any other level of understanding beyond that expanding that sort of facts that you may be able to.

A: The Isaac understanding is often detecting correlations detecting patterns. And so like push and pull is a very basic concept in physics. And then if you talk about Newton second law it creates a relation between force mass and acceleration. And so this is a correlation between concepts. It is not really something which you cannot really experience the Newton's second law in real life. You can only experience the correlation between those quantities. And so you can go to higher and higher levels of abstraction by trying to detect correlations, relations, between lower-level concepts on a higher and higher level. And so, for instance, when you study a physics textbook, we find there are at most seven, there are a total of seven levels of, levels of abstractions, there are seven levels of abstractions. And maybe this has to do with the fact that as a human, we can only remember seven things at most. I am not sure I answered your question.

